---
/**
 * website/src/components/Hero.astro — the hero (website plan, Task W1.4; changes spec of
 * 2026-10-18, items 2 and 4).
 *
 * The first screen of the site, and the one place where a marketing site is most tempted to
 * write its own words. So it writes none:
 *
 *   1. **The headline is README.md's line**, read through the content pipeline (W1.3) and
 *      rendered byte for byte — not typed in here, which is what the red step of this task
 *      was (F48, D102). It is `Lambdas for coding agents` since the changes spec of
 *      2026-10-18 (item 2), and it is the README's line that says so.
 *   2. **The subline is README.md's next line** rather than a paraphrase, for the same
 *      reason: every sentence the site publishes is traceable to a document, and a sentence
 *      written for the page is the one nobody maintains (D102, Global Constraint 6). It is
 *      the promise under the headline, the site's default description and the cards' — one
 *      line in the README, three places on the site (changes spec item 2).
 *   3. **The video slot is empty while `site.yaml` says the video does not exist** (D107):
 *      no player, no poster, no play button, nothing to click that plays nothing. W2.4 flips
 *      the flag and fills the slot in the same commit.
 *
 * **What is not here, and why.** The hero used to carry the install commands and the agent
 * chat under the promise, and item 4 asks for the picture of the run to be the page's
 * centrepiece rather than the fourth thing down: the homepage is now the promise and its
 * one call to action, then the chat, then the diagram, then the install paths
 * (`src/pages/index.astro`, which owns that order). The chat leads the three because what
 * the page offers a reader is a conversation with the agent they already have (review round
 * 5); the diagram is still the largest thing after the promise and is its own section
 * directly under the chat. The commands are
 * `HomeInstall.astro` and the chat is `AgentChat.astro`; both are placed by the page, so a
 * page can put them where its own shape wants them.
 *
 * **The one line the hero writes itself.** The headline and the promise are documents' lines;
 * the links under them are the site's own labels for what a reader does next — a link
 * label is not a sentence about the product, and there is no document that names a call to
 * action. They are held to the pages they point at by `linkcheck.mjs` like every other
 * internal link, and to the vocabulary by `website/test/wording.mjs`.
 *
 * **The console is the call to action's third link, and the flag is what renders it**
 * (changes spec of 2026-10-18, item 6). The spec asks that the console be findable from the
 * home page's call to action, and `site.yaml`'s `features.console_downloads` is what decides:
 * while it is false this component renders nothing about the console at all — no link, no
 * sentence, no placeholder — so the unreleased state is structural here exactly as it is in
 * Getting Started's console option, and the link arrives in the same commit that flips the
 * flag for the console's own release (W3.1). It points at `/console/`, the page that says
 * what the console is and carries its downloads: the hero names the way there, and the URL a
 * reader downloads from stays derived from the release in one reader
 * (`src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`, Global Constraint 9).
 */
import { loadSite } from '../content.ts'

const site = loadSite()
const facts = site.facts

// The headline: the pipeline reads README.md's tagline line, under its "# Fugaro" title,
// and it is the page's `h1` (F48, D102; changes spec item 2).
const tagline = site.tagline

// The subline: the one sentence under the headline, read from the same place and rendered
// whole — the hero's lead, the site's default description and the cards' description are
// one string in the README rather than three copies of a sentence typed here (changes spec
// item 2, D102). `website/test/home.mjs` holds this page to the README's line, and
// `website/test/layout.mjs` holds the head to the same one.
const LEAD = site.subline

// The video slot's flag. Read from site.yaml rather than assumed, so the day the video
// exists this component changes in one place and the flag is what decides (D107).
const videoExists = facts.features.video

// The console's flag, read the same way and for the same reason: the console's link is
// rendered from `site.yaml`'s `features.console_downloads` — `consoleDownloads` once
// `content.ts` has read it — and from nothing else, so the day the console is released the
// flag is the whole change on this page (changes spec item 6, W3.1).
const consoleExists = facts.features.consoleDownloads
---

<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-tagline">
  <h1 class="hero-tagline" id="hero-tagline">{tagline}</h1>

  <p class="hero-lead" data-source="README.md">{LEAD}</p>

  {/* What a reader does next: install it, or read what a run is first. The primary one is
      filled with the accent, which is the one colour on the page that means "this is the
      action" — the step numbers and the flow's rail carry the same accent — and the second
      is a quiet link, because two filled buttons are two primaries. */}
  <p class="hero-actions">
    <a class="hero-cta" href="/getting-started/">Get started</a>
    <a class="hero-cta-quiet" href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a>
    {
      // The console, while the repository says it has shipped (item 6). A quiet link like
      // "How it works" and not a second filled button: two primaries are two primaries, and
      // the console is a second way to run Fugaro rather than the thing to do next. Its
      // label is the name Getting Started's option carries, so the site calls the console
      // one thing in the two places a reader meets it.
      consoleExists && (
        <a class="hero-cta-quiet" href="/console/">
          Desktop console
        </a>
      )
    }
  </p>

  {
    videoExists && (
      <div class="hero-video" data-source="website/data/site.yaml">
        {/* W2.4 renders the explainer video here when site.yaml says it exists (D107). */}
      </div>
    )
  }
</section>

<style>
  /* Every value below is a design token (W1.2): this component names the palette, the
     spacing scale, the type scale and the radii, and defines none of them. A component that
     carried a colour of its own would be a second palette to keep in step, and a component
     that named a token the layer does not declare — a `--weight-bold` where the layer stops
     at `--weight-semibold`, say — is a style that silently does not apply at all, because an
     undefined `var()` makes the whole declaration invalid rather than falling back. Every
     name below is declared in `src/styles/tokens.css`, and `website/test/home.mjs` holds the
     built pages to that. */
  /* The hero's own gutter, and a bottom edge close enough to the diagram that the two read
     as one screen (item 4: the picture is the thing under the promise, not a section four
     screens down). `main:has(> .hero)` drops `main`'s padding on this page, so every
     top-level block here carries the gutter itself. */
  .hero {
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }

  .hero-tagline {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    /* The tagline is the page's `h1`, and on a phone it is the first thing that has to fit:
       at the desktop size (`--font-size-4xl`, 56px) the sentence runs to six lines and its
       own bottom lands below a 390 × 844 first screen, so the promise under it and the
       first install command were both below the fold (RF5's measurement, P5). The shape is
       the one the layout's own `h1` rule takes — `clamp(2xl, 6vw, …)`, so a phone gets
       `--font-size-2xl` and a wide viewport gets the rule's upper end — but the upper end
       here is the hero's own: this tagline is already the site's largest type at 56px
       (`--font-size-4xl`), and reading it at the layout's `--font-size-3xl` (44px, what
       every other page's `h1` takes above about 733px) would shrink the one loud line on
       the one page whose whole job is the promise, which is a design change and not P5's.
       What sits between 6vw and the ends is where the two rules agree; what they do at the
       wide end is the hero's, on purpose. */
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-2xl), 6vw, var(--font-size-4xl));
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .hero-lead {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* The action row: one filled link and one quiet one, on one line while there is room and
     wrapping rather than shrinking when there is not. */
  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs) var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
  }

  /* The primary: the accent as a fill and the ground as its label, which is the pair
     `a11y.mjs` computes at 4.5:1 for a label a reader reads (the same pair the flow's step
     numbers are set in). */
  .hero-cta {
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* The second action is a link, not a second button. It keeps the underline `base.css`
     gives every link in running text, so it reads as a link at a glance rather than as a
     disabled control beside the filled one. */
  .hero-cta-quiet {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
  }
</style>
